/// Cache eviction algorithms.
///
/// **`TinyLFU` is the only variant.** The LRU variant and its machinery (the RAM tier's
/// recency queue and sampled per-hit reorder, and the disk range sort's recency arm) were
/// deleted under cache-eviction-at-scale R10.1 once nothing could select them: the config
/// spelling `"lru"` resolves to `TinyLFU` in `From<config::EvictionAlgorithm>`. The enum is
/// kept rather than collapsed so the constructor signatures and `RamCacheStats` stay stable.
///
/// **`Default` is `TinyLFU`, matching [`crate::config::EvictionAlgorithm`]'s default.** The two
/// enums are converted into each other at `http_proxy.rs`'s startup wiring, so a disagreement
/// between their defaults is a disagreement about what the proxy does — and it was LRU here for a
/// while after R10.5 moved the config-facing default, on the reasoning that production always
/// converts explicitly and never reaches this `Default`. That reasoning is correct and is still
/// not a reason to leave them disagreeing: the four callers of `default()` are convenience and
/// test constructors, and a test fixture that silently exercises a different algorithm from the
/// shipped one is how a contrast stops being a contrast.
///
/// Requirement: cache-eviction-at-scale R10.1, R10.5
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize, Default)]
pub enum CacheEvictionAlgorithm {
    /// Decayed-frequency eviction: victim minimizes `(decayed_frequency(access_count,
    /// idle_secs), last_accessed)`. Access count halves once per `TINYLFU_HALF_LIFE_SECS`
    /// (1 hour) of idle time, so a frequently-accessed entry stays shielded from a single
    /// one-hit read; an idle entry's score decays toward 0 under capacity pressure. TTL
    /// expiry remains the hard ceiling for staleness — decay has no separate age ceiling.
    ///
    /// The default since 3.0.0 (R10.5).
    #[default]
    TinyLFU,
}

